# Shared review rooms

## Architecture

- **D1** (`migrations/0002_teams.sql`, append-only): `teams`, `team_members` (role `owner`/`member`), `team_uploads`. Membership is read from D1 on every request and every WebSocket handshake; there is no authorization cache.
- **`src/teams.ts`**: team, membership, team-upload and room-handshake routes, registered from `src/index.ts` and reusing the host's `requireAuth`, same-origin mutation middleware and bounded body reader. `requireAuth` now also exposes the session expiry.
- **Team uploads**: R2 key `teams/<team>/<server-uuid>`, derived from verified membership. Metadata lookups are by `(id, team_id)`, so an id from another team returns 404 and deletes nothing. Personal `/api/uploads` is unchanged.
- **Rooms**: `GET /api/teams/:team/rooms/:room/ws` checks session, room name, no query string, same Origin and current membership. It then calls the `TeamRoom` SQLite Durable Object (named `[team, room]`) through `src/routes/team-room.ts`, which drops all client headers and sends only verified identity headers. The DO has no public route. It uses `acceptWebSocket`, serialized attachments, SQL history (latest 50, `AUTOINCREMENT` seq), a `ping`/`pong` auto-response, and no socket map or timers. Messages are committed before broadcast. A per-connection token bucket allows 100 messages/s.
- **Leases**: an authorized socket lasts at most 5 minutes, capped by session expiry.
- **UI**: `/teams.html` with `public/room-chat/room-client.js` (reconnecting client; only one live connection; text rendered with `textContent`).
- The room DO and UI started from the catalog block `hibernating-websocket`, adapted to team routes.

## Read-only viewers, live revocation and retry-safe messages (follow-up)

- **Roles**: `PUT .../members/:userId` accepts `member` or `viewer`. `migrations/0003_viewer_role.sql` rebuilds `team_members` (SQLite cannot alter a CHECK), copies every row and adds `grant_id`. `0002` is untouched. Viewers can list, connect, receive and download. Upload, delete, membership changes and socket sends are refused (HTTP 403, or a `read_only` error event on the socket). The owner role is immutable (409).
- **Per-send authorization**: the gateway also passes the verified session id and membership `grant_id` to the DO. For every application send the DO queries D1 (session not revoked/expired, same team membership with the same `grant_id`, role) before validation detail, dedup lookup, conflict, ack or commit. Each recipient is authorized the same way before that send is delivered. Failing sockets are closed with 1008. The `grant_id` is created with the membership row and kept across role changes, so removing and re-adding a user does not revive their old sockets, and a revoked login session stays revoked. There are no fan-out or control routes; the DO only reads D1, so nothing new is public. The 5-minute lease remains as an upper bound.
- **Ordering**: a promise chain inside the DO serialises message handling, so commit and delivery order equals `seq`. The chain is not authoritative state. Each connection stores the highest `seq` in its snapshot to avoid a message arriving both in the snapshot and as a live event.
- **`clientId`**: optional, `[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,80}`. A new message echoes it in the `message` event and in snapshots. An exact retry by the same user in the same team/room returns `{type:"ack",seq,clientId}` to the retrying socket only. Different text yields `{type:"error",code:"conflict",clientId}`. Records live in a DO SQL `dedup` table (`userId, clientId`) that is not pruned with history and survives restarts. Old rooms are upgraded additively (`ALTER TABLE messages ADD COLUMN clientId`); legacy rows and `{text}` clients behave as before. An invalid id gets an `invalid_client_id` error event.
- **UI**: viewers get `message-send`, `message-text` and `team-upload` disabled (role read from `/api/teams` at load; a role change shows after refresh). The page sends a fresh `clientId` per user action and never resends automatically.
- Tests: `test/viewer.test.ts` (viewer HTTP/socket permissions, downgrade, removal, logout, re-add, retry concurrency, window pruning).

## Commands

`pnpm typecheck`, `pnpm test` (vitest in workerd: `test/teams.test.ts`, `test/viewer.test.ts` plus the original suites), `pnpm build` (dry-run), `pnpm db:local`.

## Limitations

- Revocation is checked at message boundaries: an idle revoked socket may stay open (it receives nothing) until the next message in the room or its lease ends. Events already authorized before a mutation response may still arrive. Each send costs D1 reads (one per distinct recipient session), a throughput trade-off.
- Dedup records are never pruned, so they grow with the number of distinct `clientId`s used.
- History is the latest 50 messages. There is no durable replay or exactly-once delivery, and the client never resends. Snapshots are replaced on reconnect.
- Membership management is API-only, for the two demo accounts only. There are no invitations, no ownership transfer and no team deletion.
- Rate limiting is per connection only, not per account or IP.
- The block's standalone Node/Miniflare tests are stubbed out (`test/team-room*.mjs`, `test/team-room.fixture.ts`) because they targeted the block's own route and needed extra dev dependencies. I had no tool to delete them. Equivalent coverage is in `test/teams.test.ts`. `public/room-chat/index.html` just redirects to `/teams.html`.
- Browser flows in `teams.html` were not exercised in a real browser here. Only the API and WebSocket paths are covered by automated tests.
